nowadays when you pay your taxes it's  done in several ways either you get a  bill at the end of the tax year or it  comes out of your paycheck before you  even see it  of course this level of bureaucratic  sophistication didn't exist during the  medieval period which raises the  question how were medieval taxes  actually collected how did a ruler  extract taxes from their subjects and  what sort of things were subject to tax  well across Europe many states at  varying levels of centralization which  affected how taxes were collected one  state which spanned thousands of miles  and also the entire medieval period was  the eastern Roman Empire often called  the Byzantine Empire and the Bumble  focus on this is because it was a well  developed state with both a large urban  and rural population so how were these  taxes actually collected well to  everyone surprised the people who bore  the brunt of taxation with the peasantry  and there were two major forms of  taxation in the Byzantine Empire the  first was rent which a peasant paid to  their local Lord in return for the right  to use land and the second was the  teller men on the household tax which  was paid to the central government rent  was based upon the value and  productivity of the land in question and  for the most part was determined by the  Lord the household tax was determined by  central tax collectors who would travel  across the Empire and take inventory of  who had what the tax collectors would  then leave and come back soon after to  collect the demanded taxes and the  responsibility lay with the Lord to make  sure it was paid but if the tax  collectors and the Lord would then make  a note of how much was paid and who paid  it to make sure the taxes weren't  collected twice and off they go  now there were three ways in which  peasants could pay their taxes  the first was simply in coin the second  was in kind like handing over the  equivalent value of their produce and  the third was via unpaid labor and this  was used for things like the  construction of public roads or churches  in case you're wondering the nobility  and the clergy were given what were  called privileges which reduced their  tax burden all of this makes it sound  like the Byzantine Empire was simply  able to tax the peasantry up to the hilt  but they did have protections the  government knew that an unhappy  unproductive peasantry would destabilize  the Empire which is why there were  limitations on how much they could be  taxed in regular peacetime that said in  times of war and war was quite common  special taxes could be levied the most  egregious of these were the farm levy an  extra tax on farm land and the seizure  of church property mostly its gold  reserves and this was a right in which  all Byzantine emperors held because  legally the entire Empire was their  personal property and everyone else only  held it with their permission it wasn't  just a peasantry  was subject to taxation another major  revenue stream for the Empire was  taxation or merchants in their trade  unlike the taxation of the peasantry  local Nobles weren't included and it was  the sole purview of the central  government and so to summarize that's  how the Byzantine government would  collect its taxes via state pay  bureaucrats who traveled the Empire to  assess land valleys and levy attacks  accordingly the Lords of the peasantry  in question would then be required to  collect the taxes and paid the next time  that the collectors were about what's  the level of Taxation vary depending on  trade location and era one thing was  consistent nobody liked paying them I  hope you enjoyed this episode and thank  you for watching with the special thanks  to my patreon supporters who you can see  on screen here and with an extra special  thanks to James Bissonnette David  archaeologist party-boy Co Danny Maloney  Colin Castleman Rob Waterhouse are in  the white Michael Reynolds archway an  emperor chris wicker Gustav Swan 